but we start this half-hour with a  development overnight in special counsel  Robert Muller's investigation into  possible links between the Trump  campaign and Russian meddling in the  2016 election the New York Times reports  Muller has asked the White House for  documents related to former national  security adviser Michael Flynn this  would be the first time the special  counsel has asked the White House for  its records it comes after Muller began  using a grand jury the sign the  investigation is intensifying here with  details on that and other aspects of the  investigation CBS News justice reporter  Paula Reed Paula good morning good  morning let's talk about this use of a  grand jury what actually does that what  does that signify here Oh grand jury is  a tool that prosecutors can use it gives  them a lot of opportunities to gather  evidence for example you can subpoena  documents you can subpoena witnesses to  come in and testify under oath and over  time once you gather that evidence then  you can assess whether or not you think  there is enough evidence to pursue  charges and if you do you can then bring  all of your evidence before the grand  jury to get an indictment it  investigative tool really for Robert  Muller how much power does he have and  and I guess broad scope does he have so  all of his power derives from the Deputy  Attorney General Rob goes inside and in  his order appointing Robert Muller he  says that he is the power to investigate  any ties between Russia and the campaign  and anything else that they uncovered  because once you start turning over  rocks in a case like this you never know  what you're going to stumble over one  great example of that is murder of  General Petraeus he was charged with  mishandling classified information that  was a cyber stalking investigation so  wherever this leads Robert Muller can  fall is there any restriction to it  there are some restrictions he has to  have some sort of evidence of a crime  for example people keep asking me about  the tax returns and you get the tax  returns well maybe if he has evidence  that there could be some sort of crime  linked to those documents he can go to a  judge and potentially get a court order  but you can't just go places out of  curiosity there has to be reason to  collect certain certain evidence when  you look at the team that Muller has put  together what does it suggest about the  direction he's going in this is a  powerhouse team decades of Justice  Department experience sterling  credentials but we are seeing are a lot  of people leaving very well-paid  jobs in the private sector and you're  not gonna do that if this is gonna be a  short time thing this is obviously going  to be a long term investigation and when  you look at the type of work these  people have done you see a lot of  white-collar crime fraud that kinds of  things we've gotten any indication  whether the grand jury has heard from  witnesses yet or if they're just  gathering evidence at this point we know  that they've been subpoenas issued for  documents and there have been interviews  of witnesses but it's not clear whether  or not those witnesses have come before  the grand jury or just been interviewed  by investigators we we often hear we've  heard the president a lot talk about the  Russia investigation in any sense  is he at risk when he talks about it  publicly in terms of what what what  advantage that might give the special  counsel it's more of a political  question really legally speaking I don't  think Robert Muller really cares too  much or it's going to be phased what the  about what the president says but  politically a lot of people in the polls  believe that this is a fair  investigation and they do have faith in  Robert Muller so if you're criticizing  him I think in the eyes of voters that  perhaps says more about you than it does  about the investigation can robert  muller call the president in front of  this grand jury this is a really  interesting question because we know  President Clinton he did give testimony  while he did not appear before the grand  jury he really had no choice that was a  unique circumstance but overwhelmingly  the preference for most defense  attorneys is to not put your client in  that situation especially if you think  they're gonna say something that's gonna  cause trouble either by telling the  truth or telling a lie so here the  president is not a trained attorney this  is starting to involve his family so  putting him in that situation it's  unlikely that any good can come of that  and it's look at his defense attorneys  will try to avoid that at all costs  I Paula Reed thanks very much for being  here this morning
 

